shrine:garh-maharaja-shorkot
  a schema:LandmarksOrHistoricalBuildings, schema:PlaceOfWorship ;
  schema:name "Garh Maharaja (Shorkot)"@en ;
  schema:description """## Overview

The shrine of Hazrat Sultan Bahu (1630–1691) at Garh Maharaja, in the Jhang region of the Punjab, honours one of the greatest of the Punjabi Sufi poet-saints and the founder of the Sarwari Qadiri order. Sultan Bahu wrote prolifically in Persian, but he is loved above all for his Punjabi *abyat* — quatrains that end each line with the long, sighing exhalation of the divine name, “Hoo” — which are sung across the Punjab in a soaring traditional style. His shrine, twice moved and rebuilt as the river Chenab shifted its course, is a major centre of pilgrimage in central Punjab.

## The Life of the Saint

Bahu was born in 1630 in the Shorkot region of Jhang, into an Awan family; his father was an official of the Mughal administration and his mother, Bibi Rasti, a woman of great piety who is herself remembered as a saint and to whom tradition credits his very name — “Bahu,” “with Hoo,” the one who is with God. Drawn from youth to the inward life, Bahu received initiation in the Qadiri order at the hand of Sayyid Abdul Rahman of Delhi, and, tradition holds, attained his spiritual completion with extraordinary swiftness. He founded his own branch of the order, the Sarwari Qadiri, which spread widely after him. A prolific author, he is credited with a great many works in Persian on the mystical path; and he composed the Punjabi verses that made him immortal. He died in 1691 and was buried in his homeland.

## The Abyat and His Teaching

Sultan Bahu’s Punjabi poetry takes the form of *si-harfi* and, most famously, the *abyat* — quatrains in which every line closes on the ecstatic syllable *Hoo* (ھُو), the sound of the breath and of the divine name, so that the poems are literally sung on the rhythm of remembrance (*dhikr*). Their theme is the burning of divine love (*ishq*), the primacy of the inward reality over outward form, and the indispensable role of the spiritual guide (*murshid*). The most famous of all begins:

اَلف اللہ چنبے دی بُوٹی، مُرشد مَن وِچ لائی ھُو

[“Alif — Allah — is a jasmine plant that my master has planted within my heart, Hoo.”]

In another he sings that a single lesson of love outweighs all the learning of the schools, and that the heart in which the Beloved dwells needs no other pilgrimage. Bahu’s verse is at once fiercely devotional and gloriously musical, and the singing of his abyat — with the long-drawn “Hoo” — is one of the characteristic sounds of Punjabi Sufi devotion.

## The Shrine

Sultan Bahu’s shrine has a history bound to the wandering of the Chenab: the river’s floods forced the tomb to be moved and rebuilt more than once, and the present shrine at Garh Maharaja is a large and much-visited complex. It keeps the round of daily prayer, qawwali and the singing of his abyat, and the free kitchen, and it draws its greatest crowds at the annual urs, held in the spring, when devotees gather in vast numbers from across the Punjab to sing his verses and seek his blessing. The Sarwari Qadiri order he founded maintains his memory and his teaching.

## Legacy

Sultan Bahu ranks with Shah Hussain, Bulleh Shah, and Waris Shah among the immortals of Punjabi poetry, and his abyat — with their unmistakable refrain of “Hoo” — are among the most widely sung of all Sufi verses in the language. As poet, prolific author, and founder of an enduring order, he shaped the spiritual life of central Punjab, and his shrine at Garh Maharaja remains a great centre of the devotion he did so much to inspire.

## Bibliography

- Alam Faqri, *Tazkirah Awliya-e-Pakistan* (Lahore) — compendium of the saints of Pakistan.
- Sultan Bahu, *Abyat-e-Bahu* (his Punjabi quatrains; verse quoted in standard published wording).
- General established histories of the Qadiri order and Punjabi Sufi poetry.

