shrine:guru-gurpat-mandir-db-80-sirey-ghat
  a schema:LandmarksOrHistoricalBuildings, schema:PlaceOfWorship ;
  schema:name "Guru Gurpat Mandir (DB-80 Sirey Ghat)"@en ;
  schema:description """## Overview

On a plot known as DB-80 in the Sirey Ghat quarter of Hyderabad, Sindh, stands the Guru Gurpat Mandir — also called the Darbar of Baba Gurpat Sahib — a Nanakpanthi shrine, registered with Pakistan's Evacuee Trust Property Board as a functional mandir, built around a Sant revered as a twelfth-generation descendant, in the Jagiasi lineage, of Guru Nanak. Unlike many of the small, poorly documented sant-shrines of upper and central Sindh, Baba Gurpat Sahib's life and the founding of his darbar are recorded with unusual specificity, principally through community memory preserved by his present-day trust and reported in recent press coverage.

## The Life of Baba Gurpat Sahib

According to the darbar's own tradition, Gurpat Sahib was born into the Jagiasi lineage of Nanakpanthi Sants — spiritual descendants of Guru Nanak through Baba Sri Chand's Udasi line — in Punjab, and travelled to Sindh in the early nineteenth century. He is said to have settled first at Khairpur during the reign of the Talpur ruler Mir Murad Ali Khan, carrying letters of introduction and support from the Sikh court of Maharaja Ranjit Singh, before moving on to Hyderabad around 1830. Among the relics he is said to have carried and enshrined were hair said to be from Baba Sri Chand, a Nanak Shahi–era rupee, and a ring attributed to Guru Gobind Singh — objects still spoken of at the darbar today. In the course of his ministry he is credited with founding several Jagiasu *tikanas* (seats) beyond Hyderabad, including at Khairpur, Halani, and Kandyaro, before his death in 1877 at the age of about seventy.

## The Darbar and Its Trust

The present building at DB-80, Sirey Ghat is dated by community record to Vikram Samvat 1988 — 1931 in the Gregorian calendar — standing on a plot of some 595 square yards, and is maintained today as a registered charitable trust (*tikkano*) whose historic trusteeship is remembered under Bao Santokdas. In addition to its worship hall, the darbar today also runs a modest vegetarian eatery and space for pilgrims to stay, with donors' names recorded on inscribed tiles — a continuation of the *langar*-adjacent hospitality long associated with Nanakpanthi and Sikh sant-shrines across Sindh.

## Community and Standing

The mandir forms part of a wider constellation of small Jagiasi and Nanakpanthi shrines scattered across the Dadu–Hyderabad belt of Sindh — the same milieu that produced the darbars of Sant Baba Bhagat Ram, Bhai Sant Thawan Das, and Gurdas Ram, all likewise registered with the Evacuee Trust Property Board. Its comparatively rich documentation — a named founder, a dated building, and a preserved lineage — makes it one of the better-attested sites in this cluster, even as the deeper historical record beyond community memory remains thin.

## A Note on the Sources

Much of what is recorded here rests on the darbar's own community tradition as related in recent press coverage rather than independent archival verification. The identification of the ETPB-registered "Guru Gurpat Mandir, DB-80 Sirey Ghat" with the Baba Gurpat Sahib darbar described in that coverage is highly probable — same name, same locality — but not explicitly cross-confirmed in a single authoritative source. Dates and figures should be read with this in mind.

## Bibliography

- The Friday Times, "At The Darbar Of Baba Gurpat Sahib In Hyderabad" (26 January 2025).
- Evacuee Trust Property Board (Pakistan), Functional Mandirs register.
- General accounts of the Jagiasi/Udasi Nanakpanthi tradition in Sindh.

