shrine:sadh-belo-sadh-belo-island-temple
  a schema:LandmarksOrHistoricalBuildings, schema:PlaceOfWorship ;
  schema:name "Sadh Belo (Sadh Belo Island Temple)"@en ;
  schema:description """## Overview

Sadh Belo is a serene temple complex occupying a wooded island in the River Indus, set between the twin cities of Sukkur and Rohri in Sindh. One of the most important living centres of Hindu worship in the province, it is intimately bound to the *Udasi* tradition, the ascetic order that traces its lineage to Sri Chand, elder son of Guru Nanak, and so stands at a fascinating confluence of Hindu and Sikh devotion. Spread across two interconnected islets, the complex encompasses temples, shrines, *samadhis* (memorial tombs of holy men), gardens, a communal kitchen and living quarters, all of it founded in the early nineteenth century amid the palms and greenery that give the island its name.

## History and the Coming of Baba Bankhandi

The founding of Sadh Belo is attributed to the *Udasi* saint Baba Bankhandi Maharaj, born Balchand Sharma, whose origins are variously traced, according to differing accounts, to Kero Khetar near Delhi or to Nepal. As a young spiritual seeker, still in his teens, he is said to have arrived in Sukkur, then a flourishing trading hub of the subcontinent, in the year 1823. Drawn to the tranquil, forested island in the Indus, he settled there and made it a place of meditation and worship, and it came to be known as *Sadhu Belo*, meaning "the woods of the sage". Under Baba Bankhandi and the succession of *gaddi nashin* (spiritual successors) who followed him, the island grew into a substantial religious establishment.

The complex enshrines a broad and characteristically syncretic array of devotion. Baba Bankhandi is credited with establishing places of worship dedicated to Hindu deities including Annapurna, Hanuman, Ganesh and Shiv Shankar, while also making provision for the veneration of the *Guru Granth Sahib* and the *Bhagavad Gita* side by side, an expression of the inclusive *Udasi* ethos. Much of the fine architecture visible today, including the principal temple, was raised somewhat later; the main shrine is generally dated to the closing years of the nineteenth century, built under an eighth successor in the lineage, Sant Harnam Das, and further embellished in subsequent decades.

## The Udasi Tradition and the Island Complex

The distinctive character of Sadh Belo flows from its rootedness in the *Udasipanth*. This religious and ascetic movement grew from the teachings of Sri Chand, the elder son of Guru Nanak, and blended devotional streams in a manner that resists neat sectarian classification, honouring Sikh scripture and Hindu deities alike, and cultivating a tradition of renunciation, pilgrimage and hospitality. Sadh Belo became one of the pre-eminent seats of *Udasi* religious life in Sindh.

The island itself is arranged to serve both worship and community. The principal island, Sadh Belo, houses the temples, verandahs and the *langar*-style kitchen that fed pilgrims and residents, while the adjoining island, sometimes called Deen Belo, contains *samadhis*, a park and the Rishi Nol *mandir*. Reached by boat across the Indus, the complex offered pilgrims a self-contained sacred world amid the river, its white temples and shaded courtyards forming a striking contrast to the surrounding waters.

## Partition, Survival and Pilgrimage

The Partition of 1947 dispersed much of Sindh's Hindu population and cast uncertainty over its many shrines, yet Sadh Belo endured as one of the most significant functioning Hindu sites in Pakistan. It has continued to be maintained by its custodians and to draw devotees, and it remains the focus of an annual festival that gathers Hindus from across Sindh and beyond, when boats ferry pilgrims to the island for days of worship, music and communal meals. Its survival has made it a cherished anchor for the Hindu community of the region and a rare instance of continuity across the rupture of Partition.

## Significance Today

Sadh Belo stands as a remarkable testament to Sindh's plural religious heritage and to the enduring vitality of the *Udasi* tradition on the banks of the Indus. As both a place of active pilgrimage and a monument to the syncretic devotion of Baba Bankhandi and his successors, the island temple remains one of the most beloved and evocative sacred sites of Hindu Sindh, its shrines rising from the river much as they have for two centuries.

## Bibliography

- Documentation on Sadh Belo and its founding in surveys of the heritage of Sindh.
- Studies of the *Udasi* order and the legacy of Sri Chand in Sindh.
- Feature reporting on Sadh Belo and its annual festival (Dawn; Scroll.in; *Daily Times*).
- Regional histories of Hindu Sindh and the shrines of the Sukkur–Rohri Indus corridor.
- Accounts of the *gaddi nashin* lineage and the nineteenth-century building of the temple complex.

