shrine:shrine-of-makhdoom-jahaniyan-jahangasht
  a schema:LandmarksOrHistoricalBuildings, schema:PlaceOfWorship ;
  schema:name "Shrine of Makhdoom Jahaniyan Jahangasht"@en ;
  schema:description """## Overview

At Uch Sharif rests Sayyid Jalaluddin Bukhari (1308–1384), universally known by his honorifics *Makhdoom-i-Jahaniyan* — “the Master of the World” — and *Jahangasht*, “the World-Traveller.” Grandson of Jalaluddin Surkh-Posh Bukhari, he was among the most learned and widely revered Suhrawardi saints of fourteenth-century India, a tireless traveller and preacher whose authority was sought by sultans and commoners alike. His shrine is one of the principal darbars of Uch Sharif and a major centre of pilgrimage in the southern Punjab.

## The Life of the Saint

Born at Uch in 1308 into the Bukhari sayyid family founded by his grandfather Surkh-Posh, Jalaluddin was schooled in the religious sciences and the Suhrawardi discipline and grew into a scholar-saint of vast reputation. He earned the title *Jahangasht* — “he who has roamed the world” — through his extraordinary travels: he journeyed across the Muslim world, performed the Hajj many times, and visited the great centres of learning and sanctity of his age, gathering knowledge and spiritual connection wherever he went. Honoured as *Makhdoom-i-Jahaniyan*, he was held in the highest esteem by the rulers of the Delhi Sultanate, particularly in the Tughluq age, and his counsel and blessing were widely sought. He died in 1384 and was buried at Uch, where his shrine became a focus of devotion.

## Influence and the Bukhari Line

Makhdoom Jahaniyan Jahangasht carried the prestige of the Uch Bukhari sayyids to its height. A prolific guide with a wide following, he strengthened the Suhrawardi presence across the Punjab and Sind, and his descendants and disciples spread the *Jalali* Bukhari line far and wide — a lineage of sayyids who would remain religiously and politically prominent for centuries and whose branches reached into many regions of the subcontinent. His memory is bound to that of his grandfather Surkh-Posh and to the whole flowering of sanctity that made Uch Sharif a spiritual capital of the south.

## The Shrine and its Devotion

The shrine of Makhdoom Jahaniyan Jahangasht at Uch Sharif is a busy darbar amid the town’s famous complex of tombs, kept by his descendants and visited year-round by pilgrims seeking the blessing of the “Master of the World.” It maintains the customary devotions — prayer, qawwali, and the free kitchen — and its annual urs draws crowds from across the region. It stands close to the celebrated tiled mausolea of Uch, including that of his descendant Bibi Jawindi, in one of Pakistan’s richest concentrations of sacred architecture.

## Legacy

Makhdoom Jahaniyan Jahangasht is remembered as one of the great Suhrawardi saints of medieval India — the learned, far-travelled “Master of the World” whose authority spanned the Sultanate and whose lineage shaped the religious landscape of the Punjab and Sind for generations. His shrine at Uch Sharif keeps alive the memory of the age when Uch, with Multan, was a beating heart of Sufism in the subcontinent.

## Bibliography

- Alam Faqri, *Tazkirah Awliya-e-Pakistan* (Lahore) — compendium of the saints of Pakistan.
- General established histories of the Suhrawardi order and of Uch Sharif.

