shrine:shrine-of-mauj-darya-bukhari
  a schema:LandmarksOrHistoricalBuildings, schema:PlaceOfWorship ;
  schema:name "Shrine of Mauj Darya Bukhari"@en ;
  schema:description """Tucked away on Edward Road near the old Customs House in Anarkali, the octagonal tomb of Hazrat Meeran Muhammad Shah Bukhari — popularly called "Mauj Darya," or "Wave of the River" — commemorates a saint born in 1533 in Uch Sharif, a descendant of Jalaluddin Surkh-Posh Bukhari and an adherent of the Suhrawardi silsila founded by Bahauddin Zakariya of Multan. He became a hafiz-e-Quran in his youth and, according to popular tradition, earned his epithet by miraculously diverting the flood-prone River Ravi away from Lahore. He is remembered as a spiritual confidant of the Mughal emperor Akbar, reportedly advising him during the siege of Chittor Fort, and legend credits him with distributing to the poor a substantial reward Akbar granted him near Batala. Architecturally the tomb is unusual among Lahore's shrines for its octagonal plan, with a low dome, corner towers at each of its eight angles, and a mixture of recessed blind arches and plain panelled façades — a style thought to echo Multani funerary tradition. His first wife, Mai Fatima, rests in a separate shrine facing his own, near the historic Jain Mandir. The shrine gained unwelcome national attention in 2018 when construction of the Orange Line Metro Train's underground Anarkali station demolished its mosque, langar khana and courtyard, with tunnelling reported to pass close to the saint's grave in apparent violation of heritage buffer-zone regulations — an episode that became a flashpoint in Lahore's debates over balancing infrastructure development against protection of religious heritage. The shrine remains one of scores of Lahore shrines administered by the Punjab Auqaf and Religious Affairs Department, and continues to draw a devoted following, particularly for its Thursday evening gatherings.

