shrine:shrine-of-peer-makki
  a schema:LandmarksOrHistoricalBuildings, schema:PlaceOfWorship ;
  schema:name "Shrine of Peer Makki"@en ;
  schema:description """Standing close to the great mausoleum of Data Ganj Bakhsh, in the Walled City's Bhati Gate quarter, the shrine of Peer Makki commemorates Hazrat Syed Azizuddin al-Hassani wal-Hussaini, a scholar-mystic born in Baghdad who reportedly spent twelve years in ascetic seclusion at Mecca — hence his popular title "Makki." Hagiographical accounts hold that he arrived in Lahore around 1178 CE by what tradition describes as divine instruction, and remained for some thirty-six years as a teacher of both the exoteric sciences of Islamic law and the esoteric path of the Junaidi Sufi lineage, tracing back to the classical Baghdad master Junayd. He is said to have died in 1215 CE. A well-loved anecdote recalls that during Sultan Muhammad Ghori's 1178 siege of Lahore, the besieged governor Khusro Malik sought Peer Makki's prayers, and the saint assured him of six further years of safety. Devotional custom in Lahore holds that pilgrims should pay their respects at Peer Makki's tomb before proceeding to Data Darbar, honouring him as a spiritual forerunner in the same sacred locale — although the popular claim that he personally tutored Data Ganj Bakhsh sits awkwardly with the conventional dating of Ali Hujwiri's death around 1072, a century before Peer Makki's own arrival, and should be understood as devotional tradition rather than strict chronology. The shrine complex includes an adjoining mosque, the Hazrat Pir Makki Masjid, and is formally administered, like Data Darbar itself, by the Punjab Auqaf and Religious Affairs Department. Despite its proximity to one of Pakistan's most visited shrines, Peer Makki's own tomb receives markedly fewer pilgrims, giving it a more intimate, contemplative atmosphere even during its annual urs.

====================================================================================="""@en ;
  schema:image <https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Hazrat_Pir_Makki_Masjid.JPG> ;
