  a schema:LandmarksOrHistoricalBuildings, schema:PlaceOfWorship ;
  schema:name "Shrine of Shah Jamal"@en ;
  schema:description """The shrine of Baba Shah Jamal, in the locality that now bears his name adjoining Ichhra and opposite Forman Christian College, commemorates Syed Shah Jamal, a Sufi of Hussaini Syed descent who traced his lineage to Jalaluddin Surkh-Posh Bukhari of Uch Sharif. He belonged jointly to the Qadiriyya, Suhrawardiyya and Chishtiyya orders, and lived in Lahore during the reign of the Mughal emperor Akbar, whose syncretic Din-i-Ilahi he is remembered for having resisted, working instead to draw people back towards orthodox Islamic practice. He is generally held to have died in the later seventeenth century, though sources differ on the precise year (1649 and 1671 both appear). Today the shrine is best known not for quiet contemplation but for its exuberant Thursday-night dhamal, when devotees, students, musicians and curious visitors gather from dusk until the early hours as dhol drummers — most famously the late Pappu Sain, whose successors continue the tradition — drive crowds into rhythmic, trance-like dancing. This weekly spectacle has made Shah Jamal one of the most written-about expressions of popular Sufi devotional culture in Punjab. The tomb itself is enclosed within a mosque of relatively modern construction, single-domed with four minarets, reflecting rebuilding rather than preserved Mughal fabric; an adjoining graveyard lies beside it. The current sajjada nashin, a descendant of the saint, continues to hold Khatam Khawajgan ceremonies. The site illustrates the continuing vitality of shrine-centred devotional practice in Lahore, even as questions over noise, crowding and substance use among some attendees have periodically drawn official scrutiny.

